  "text": "We are given the function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6}{x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1}\n$$\n\nWe are to find all real values of $ x $ such that:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Set up the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6}{x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1} = 1\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6 = x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1 - x^3 + 6x^2 - 11x + 6 = 0\n$$\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n- $ x^4 $\n- $ -2x^3 - x^3 = -3x^3 $\n- $ x^2 + 6x^2 = 7x^2 $\n- $ -2x - 11x = -13x $\n- Constant: $ +1 + 6 = 7 $\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Solve the quartic equation\n\nWe are looking for real values of $ x $ that satisfy the quartic equation:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7 = 0\n$$\n\nThis is a **quartic** equation, and solving quartic equations analytically is generally difficult, so we look for rational roots using the Rational Root Theorem.\n\nThe possible rational roots are factors of the constant term over the leading coefficient:\n\n- ±1, ±7\n\nLet's test $ x = 1 $:\n\n$$\n1^4 - 3(1)^3 + 7(1)^2 - 13(1) + 7 = 1 - 3 + 7 - 13 + 7 = -1 = 0 \\quad \\text{(Not a root)}\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 7 $:\n\n$$\n7^4 - 3(7)^3 + 7(7)^2 - 13(7) + 7 = 2401 - 1029 + 343 - 91 + 7 = 1400 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 7 $ again — nope.\n\nTry $ x = 1/1 = 1 $ — already tried.\n\nTry $ x = -1 $:\n\n$$\n(-1)^4 - 3(-1)^3 + 7(-1)^2 - 13(-1) + 7 = 1 + 3 + 7 + 13 + 7 = 30 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = -7 $:\n\n$$\n(-7)^4 - 3(-7)^3 + 7(-7)^2 - 13(-7) + 7 = 2401 + 882 + 343 + 91 + 7 = 3624 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 7 $ again — nope.\n\nTry $ x = 1 $ again — nope.\n\nTry $ x = 2 $:\n\n$$\n2^4 - 3(2)^3 + 7(2)^2 - 13(2) + 7 = 16 - 24 + 28 - 26 + 7 = 3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 3 $:\n\n$$\n81 - 81 + 63 - 39 + 7 = 20 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = -2 $:\n\n$$\n16 + 24 + 28 + 26 + 7 = 91 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 7 $ — nope.\n\nTry $ x = 1/7 $:\n\n$$\n(1/7)^4 - 3(1/7)^3 + 7(1/7)^2 - 13(1/7) + 7\n$$\n\nThis is messy, and likely not a rational root.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Consider that the original function might have a factor that can be factored or simplify.\n\nLet’s look at the denominator:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1\n$$\n\nTry factoring this denominator:\n\nLet’s try $ x = 1 $:\n\n$$\n1 - 2 + 1 - 2 + 1 = -1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = -1 $:\n\n$$\n1 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 7 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 2 $:\n\n$$\n16 - 16 + 4 - 4 + 1 = 1 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 3 $:\n\n$$\n81 - 54 + 9 - 6 + 1 = 31 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 4 $:\n\n$$\n256 - 128 + 16 - 8 + 1 = 137 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 1/2 $:\n\n$$\n(1/16) - (3/8) + (7/4) - (13/2) + 1\n$$\n\nThis is messy, and likely not a rational root.\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Try factoring the quartic\n\nWe can try to factor the quartic:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7\n$$\n\nLet’s try grouping:\n\nTry grouping:\n\n$$\n(x^4 - 3x^3) + (7x^2 - 13x + 7)\n$$\n\nFactor:\n\n$$\nx^3(x - 3) + (7x^2 - 13x + 7)\n$$\n\nTry factoring the quadratic:\n\n$$\n7x^2 - 13x + 7\n$$\n\nDiscriminant:\n\n$$\n(-13)^2 - 4(7)(7) = 169 - 196 = -27 < 0\n$$\n\nSo, it doesn’t factor into real polynomials.\n\nSo, the quartic does not factor nicely. We need to find its real roots numerically or through other means.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Use substitution or graphing\n\nLet’s define:\n\n$$\ng(x) = f(x) - 1 = \\frac{x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6}{x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1} - 1 = \\frac{x^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6 - (x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1)}{x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1}\n$$\n\nSimplify numerator:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 11x - 6 - x^4 + 2x^3 - x^2 + 2x - 1 = -x^4 + 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\frac{-x^4 + 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7}{x^4 - 2x^3 + x^2 - 2x + 1}\n$$\n\nSet $ g(x) = 0 $:\n\n$$\n-x^4 + 3x^3 + 7x^2 - 13x + 7 = 0\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by $ -1 $:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 - 7x^2 + 13x - 7 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for real solutions to:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 - 7x^2 + 13x - 7 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 6: Try rational roots again\n\nTry $ x = 1 $:\n\n$$\n1 - 3 - 7 + 13 - 7 = -4 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 7 $:\n\n$$\n7^4 - 3(7)^3 - 7(7)^2 + 13(7) - 7 = 2401 - 1029 - 343 + 91 - 7 = 1135 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 2 $:\n\n$$\n16 - 24 - 28 + 26 - 7 = -17 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 3 $:\n\n$$\n81 - 81 - 63 + 39 - 7 = -30 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 4 $:\n\n$$\n256 - 192 - 112 + 52 - 7 = 35 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nTry $ x = 1.5 $:\n\n$$\n(1.5)^4 - 3(1.5)^3 - 7(1.5)^2 + 13(1.5) - 7\n$$\n\n$$\n= 5.0625 - 3(3.375) - 7(2.25) + 19.5 - 7\n$$\n\n$$\n= 5.0625 - 10.125 - 15.75 + 19.5 - 7 = 0\n$$\n\nSo, $ x = 1.5 $ is a root.\n\n---\n\n### Step 7: Factor the polynomial\n\nLet’s factor $ x^4 - 3x^3 - 7x^2 + 13x - 7 $ with $ x = 1.5 $.\n\nLet’s write:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nLet $ x = \\frac{3}{2} $, then the equation becomes 0.\n\nSo, we can factor:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(x^3 + \\text{something} \\right) = 0\n$$\n\nLet’s perform polynomial division or factorization.\n\nActually, we already know $ x = \\frac{3}{2} $ is a root.\n\nWe can divide the quartic by $ x - \\frac{3}{2} $:\n\nLet’s write:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 - 7x^2 + 13x - 7\n$$\n\nLet’s divide by $ x - \\frac{3}{2} $.\n\nUsing synthetic division:\n\nLet’s write:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r|rrrrr}\n\\frac{3}{2} & 1 & -3 & -7 & 13 & -7 \\\\\n\\hline\n & & \\frac{9}{2} & \\frac{9}{2} & -\\frac{13}{2} & \\frac{13}{2} \\\\\n\\hline\n & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{9}{2} & \\frac{9}{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo the quotient is:\n\n$$\nx^3 + \\frac{9}{2}x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}x + 0\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\nx^4 - 3x^3 - 7x^2 + 13x - 7 = (x - \\frac{3}{2})\\left(x^3 + \\frac{9}{2}x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}x\\right)\n$$\n\nFactor further:\n\n$$\nx^3 + \\frac{9}{2}x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}x = x\\left(x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}x + \\frac{9}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nSo the equation factors as:\n\n$$\n(x - \\frac{3}{2})x\\left(x^2 + \\frac{9}{2}x + \\frac{9}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nSo the real solutions are:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{3}{2}, \\quad x = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\left\\{ \\frac{3}{2}, 0 \\right\\}}\n$$",
